| R9 look | PASS | head faces direction of travel, body segments, red apple, checker board, green title logo, red-framed game-over panel: distinct and consistent |

## Feel (player's view)
- Starting is obvious; the board/snake is faintly visible behind the title veil, which is fine.
- Pace: 130 ms/tick is comfortable for the first points; speed-up of 2 ms/point is gentle (70 ms floor reached only at 30 points).
- Controls responsive; turns are buffered (max 2 queued). Quick U-turn manoeuvres work when keys are ~one tick apart.
- Replay guard: "play again" text appears only after 0.4 s, and Enter inside that window is ignored — I did not find it annoying; it prevents accidental restarts.
- Pause (P) works with its own screen; switching away (blur) auto-pauses — good.

## Minor observations (none block release; none violate R1–R8)
1. **Third key in a burst is dropped.** Pressing Down, Left, Up within one tick keeps only the first two (`queue` = down,left). Intentional cap, only noticeable on very fast combos (tight U-turn escapes). Repro: `self2.py`, burst section.
2. **Death scene is hidden by the 80 % veil** on the game-over screen; the head partly sits under the panel/veil so the player can't see clearly what they hit. Cosmetic.
3. **Wall has no distinct look**: the bounded board is delimited only by a thin border line; a player cannot see a "wall" tile. Readable, but R2's "visible, bounded grid" is met only modestly.
4. Sound unverified by ear (see limits); music loop/turn sound start only after the first key press, as browsers require.
5. Title help line and in-page footer ("Arrow keys: turn · Enter / Space: start · P: pause · M: sound") are small/low-contrast.

## Verdict
Playable start to finish and matches the objective on every point. **Nothing found that should block release.**
